From 01cb44a3468961d4fd5d79293ff0ad826c1959f7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 07:10:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Tell Apple's too many requests apart from its answers

SideSign read a body before looking at its HTTP status. When Apple
limits the sign-ins for now, it answers HTTP 429, Too Many Requests,
with a page of its web server's for a body; read as a property list,
that page became "Invalid server response: unparseable format" with
the page's HTML for words, which the player saw as it was. Cartouche's
anisette also took that error for a machine Apple refused, so the next
sign-in made a new machine, provisioned it and asked for one more
verification code: more requests, while Apple asked for fewer.

Before reading a body, the sign-in, its verification code and the
developer services now throw ServerError.httpStatus for Apple's 429,
whatever its body, and for another status of error with a page for a
body. The error keeps the status, the seconds Retry-After asks to wait
(a week at most) and which of Apple's services answered (grandSlam,
developerServices, other); never the page, nor its words. Any other
answer is read as before, with the codes of Apple's own answers.

A 429 is no longer a refusal of the anisette machine: Apple limits the
account or the network, whatever the machine, which is kept. Another
status with a page still is, as before.

Cartouche's outcome (cartouche.outcome, patch 0014) gives the status
as http, and the service and the wait as service and retryAfter, for
Cartouche to tell the player in its own words, wait before signing in
again, and send them in its measurements.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
